drills\", \"Audiolingual method\", \"Phonics-focused lessons\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Content-based instruction teaches language through meaningful subject matter, integrating both goals.\",\n      \"Grammar drills isolate language forms from content.\",\n      \"Audiolingual method focuses on pattern practice, not content.\",\n      \"Phonics focuses on decoding, not content learning.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  }\n  ,\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q401\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which level of language focuses on the arrangement of words into phrases and clauses?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Phonology\", \"Morphology\", \"Syntax\", \"Semantics\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Phonology studies sound systems, not word arrangement.\",\n      \"Morphology studies word formation from morphemes, not sentence structure.\",\n      \"Correct! Syntax governs how words combine into phrases, clauses, and sentences.\",\n      \"Semantics studies meaning, not grammatical structure.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q402\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Identifying prefixes and suffixes to determine word formation is primarily a task in:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Phonology\", \"Morphology\", \"Semantics\", \"Pragmatics\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Phonology deals with sounds, not word parts.\",\n      \"Correct! Morphology analyzes word structure and formation from morphemes (roots, prefixes, suffixes).\",\n      \"Semantics addresses meaning, not word structure.\",\n      \"Pragmatics examines context and use, not word formation.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q403\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"A descriptive grammar statement would MOST likely say:\",\n    \"options\": [\"'Never split infinitives.'\", \"'People often end sentences with prepositions in casual speech.'\", \"'Don't use double negatives.'\", \"'Always use whom as the object.'\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"This is prescriptive—dictating what not to do.\",\n      \"Correct! Descriptive grammar observes actual language use without judgment.\",\n      \"This is prescriptive—dictating a rule.\",\n      \"This is prescriptive—dictating correct form.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q404\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"A line that can be interpreted in multiple ways demonstrates:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Denotation\", \"Ambiguity\", \"Oxymoron\", \"Cliché\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Denotation is literal meaning, not multiple interpretations.\",\n      \"Correct! Ambiguity allows multiple valid interpretations.\",\n      \"Oxymoron combines contradictory terms, not creates multiple meanings.\",\n      \"Cliché is overused expression, not multiple interpretations.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q405\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which device best creates sensory impressions in readers?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Allusion\", \"Imagery\", \"Synecdoche\", \"Apostrophe\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Allusion references other texts, not sensory details.\",\n      \"Correct! Imagery uses sensory language (sight, sound, smell, taste, touch) to evoke experiences.\",\n      \"Synecdoche uses parts for wholes, not sensory details.\",\n      \"Apostrophe addresses absent entities, not creates sensory impressions.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q406\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Connotation refers to:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Dictionary meaning\", \"Spelling variants\", \"Associated meanings and feelings\", \"Syntactic function\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Dictionary meaning is denotation, not connotation.\",\n      \"Spelling variants are orthographic, not connotative.\",\n      \"Correct! Connotation is the emotional/cultural associations beyond literal meaning.\",\n      \"Syntactic function is grammatical role, not meaning associations.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q407\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"A poet uses slant rhyme and irregular meter. The most likely effect is to:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Create a monotonous rhythm\", \"Heighten tension or unease\", \"Ensure strict symmetry\", \"Eliminate ambiguity\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Irregular meter disrupts monotony rather than creates it.\",\n      \"Correct! Slant rhyme and irregular meter create tension and disrupt expectations, producing unease.\",\n      \"Irregular meter breaks symmetry, not ensures it.\",\n      \"These techniques can increase ambiguity, not eliminate it.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 3\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q408\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which choice best signals a shift in tone that creates ironic undertones?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Literal description only\", \"Exaggerated praise of a clearly flawed subject\", \"Chronological exposition\", \"Objective reporting\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Literal description lacks the contrast needed for irony.\",\n      \"Correct! Exaggerating praise of something flawed creates verbal irony—saying opposite of what's meant.\",\n      \"Chronological structure doesn't create irony.\",\n      \"Objective reporting avoids ironic tone.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q409\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Dialogue that reveals a character's values is primarily an example of:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Plot structure\", \"Characterization\", \"Setting detail\", \"Foreshadowing\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Plot is sequence of events, not character revelation.\",\n      \"Correct! Characterization methods include dialogue revealing personality, beliefs, and values.\",\n      \"Setting is time/place, not character traits.\",\n      \"Foreshadowing hints at future events, not character values.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q410\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"A passage describing storm clouds gathering over a quiet town most directly develops:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Setting and mood\", \"Tone and theme\", \"Character arc\", \"Point of view\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Setting (weather, location) and mood (ominous atmosphere) are developed through environmental description.\",\n      \"Tone is author's attitude; theme is central idea—neither directly developed here.\",\n      \"Character arc is personal growth trajectory, not environmental description.\",\n      \"Point of view is narrative perspective, not setting/mood.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q411\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which is the BEST indicator of a universal theme?\",\n    \"options\": [\"A proper noun repeated throughout\", \"A moral relevant only to one era\", \"A concept resonating across cultures and time\", \"A specific regional dialect\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Repeated names don't indicate universal significance.\",\n      \"Era-specific morals aren't universal.\",\n      \"Correct! Universal themes address fundamental human experiences that transcend culture and time.\",\n      \"Regional dialect is specific, not universal.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q412\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"The strongest thesis statement is typically:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Vague and broad\", \"A factual statement\", \"A clear, arguable claim with scope\", \"A rhetorical question\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Vague, broad statements lack focus and arguability.\",\n      \"Facts aren't arguable—strong theses present debatable positions.\",\n      \"Correct! Strong theses are clear, arguable, specific, and provide direction for the essay.\",\n      \"Questions don't assert positions—they invite inquiry.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q413\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which organizational pattern is best for evaluating two pedagogical models?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Chronological\", \"Definition\", \"Compare–contrast\", \"Process analysis\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Chronological shows time sequence, not evaluation.\",\n      \"Definition explains terms, not compares models.\",\n      \"Correct! Compare-contrast examines similarities and differences—ideal for evaluating two approaches.\",\n      \"Process analysis explains how something works, not compares models.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q414\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"A news article most appropriately uses which structure?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Inverted pyramid with most important info first\", \"Circular structure with motif return\", \"Five-act dramatic arc\", \"Stream-of-consciousness\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! News articles use inverted pyramid—crucial information first, details following in decreasing importance.\",\n      \"Circular structure is literary, not journalistic.\",\n      \"Five-act structure is for drama, not news.\",\n      \"Stream-of-consciousness is experimental fiction, not journalism.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q415\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which revision MOST improves coherence in a formal essay?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Adding varied fonts\", \"Replacing academic terms with slang\", \"Adding transitional phrases that clarify logic\", \"Removing topic sentences\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Font variation is visual, not logical coherence.\",\n      \"Slang reduces formality and precision, not improves coherence.\",\n      \"Correct! Transitions (however, therefore, consequently) clarify logical relationships between ideas.\",\n      \"Topic sentences guide readers—removing them reduces coherence.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q416\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"A business letter should prioritize:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Personal storytelling and figurative language\", \"Casual tone and slang\", \"Clear purpose, concise paragraphs, formal register\", \"Second-person commands throughout\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Business letters should be professional, not personal or figurative.\",\n      \"Casual tone/slang lacks professionalism.\",\n      \"Correct! Business letters require clarity, brevity, formal register, and professional tone.\",\n      \"Commands can be appropriate but aren't the priority—clarity and formality are.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q417\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\n    \"stem\": \"In an expository essay, which evidence is MOST appropriate to support a data-heavy claim?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Vivid sensory description\", \"Peer-reviewed research and credible statistics\", \"Personal anecdote only\", \"Fictional dialogue\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Sensory description is literary, not analytical evidence.\",\n      \"Correct! Data-heavy claims require credible, factual evidence from reliable sources.\",\n      \"Personal anecdotes alone lack the authority of research.\",\n      \"Fictional dialogue is creative, not factual evidence.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  }\n  ,\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q601\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Using surrounding sentences to infer an unfamiliar word's meaning demonstrates:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Phonetic decoding\", \"Context clue analysis\", \"Syntax identification\", \"Summarizing\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Phonetic decoding uses sound-letter relationships, not context.\",\n      \"Correct! Context clue analysis uses surrounding words and sentences to determine meaning.\",\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not meaning inference.\",\n      \"Summarizing condenses main ideas, not word meanings.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q602\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which reading technique would best locate a specific statistic in a long article?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Skimming\", \"Scanning\", \"Predicting\", \"Annotating\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Skimming finds main ideas, not specific details.\",\n      \"Correct! Scanning rapidly searches for specific information like statistics or names.\",\n      \"Predicting anticipates content, not locates existing details.\",\n      \"Annotating marks text but doesn't specifically locate information.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q603\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"A reader slowing down for complex sections and speeding through easier ones demonstrates:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Skimming\", \"Variable reading rate\", \"Metacognitive regulation\", \"Contextual inference\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Skimming is always fast, not variable based on difficulty.\",\n      \"While the rate varies, the key is awareness and adjustment.\",\n      \"Correct! Metacognitive regulation monitors comprehension and adjusts strategies accordingly.\",\n      \"Contextual inference determines meaning, not reading speed adjustment.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q604\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Identifying whether a statement expresses a general idea or a supporting detail aids:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Summarization\", \"Inference\", \"Fact-checking\", \"Syntax analysis\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Distinguishing main ideas from details is essential for effective summarization.\",\n      \"Inference draws conclusions, not identifies text structure.\",\n      \"Fact-checking verifies accuracy, not text structure.\",\n      \"Syntax analyzes sentence structure, not idea hierarchy.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q605\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"A reader connecting new material to personal experience is applying:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Phonemic awareness\", \"Prior knowledge activation\", \"Context clue analysis\", \"Morphological decoding\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Phonemic awareness is sound manipulation, not personal connections.\",\n      \"Correct! Prior knowledge activation links new information to existing knowledge for better comprehension.\",\n      \"Context clues determine word meanings, not personal connections.\",\n      \"Morphological decoding analyzes word structure, not personal experience.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q606\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which question best promotes critical analysis of a persuasive text?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Who wrote this and why?\", \"What is the spelling pattern?\", \"How many pages is it?\", \"What font is used?\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Identifying author and purpose is fundamental to critical analysis of persuasive texts.\",\n      \"Spelling patterns are orthographic, not critical analysis.\",\n      \"Page count is irrelevant to analysis.\",\n      \"Font choice is aesthetic, not analytical.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q607\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"When a text appeals to emotions to influence readers, it primarily uses:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Logos\", \"Ethos\", \"Pathos\", \"Syntax\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Logos appeals to logic and reason, not emotions.\",\n      \"Ethos establishes credibility, not emotional appeal.\",\n      \"Correct! Pathos appeals to emotions (fear, sympathy, anger) to persuade.\",\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not a persuasive appeal.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q608\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Determining if an author's evidence sufficiently supports a claim evaluates:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Relevance and sufficiency\", \"Tone and style\", \"Syntax and diction\", \"Word choice and connotation\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Evaluating evidence requires assessing if it's relevant (related) and sufficient (adequate amount).\",\n      \"Tone and style affect presentation, not evidence quality.\",\n      \"Syntax and diction are language choices, not evidence evaluation.\",\n      \"Word choice affects meaning but doesn't evaluate evidence support.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q609\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Recognizing the difference between 'Scientists discovered' and 'Scientists believe' shows awareness of:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Bias\", \"Syntax\", \"Imagery\", \"Metaphor\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Word choice revealing certainty vs. interpretation indicates bias or objectivity.\",\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not word choice implications.\",\n      \"Imagery creates sensory impressions, not relevant here.\",\n      \"Metaphor is figurative comparison, not relevant here.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q610\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"A consumer comparing ingredient lists and company claims practices:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Critical reading of informational texts\", \"Predictive inference\", \"Summative reasoning\", \"Literal comprehension\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Critical reading evaluates information for accuracy, bias, and purpose—essential for consumer literacy.\",\n      \"Predictive inference anticipates outcomes, not evaluates claims.\",\n      \"Summative reasoning is synthesis, but critical evaluation is more specific.\",\n      \"Literal comprehension understands surface meaning, not evaluates critically.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q611\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"A workplace memo should be evaluated for:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Figurative language\", \"Organization and accuracy\", \"Rhythm and meter\", \"Bias and irony\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Memos use literal, not figurative language.\",\n      \"Correct! Workplace documents prioritize clear organization and factual accuracy.\",\n      \"Rhythm and meter are poetic devices, not workplace writing criteria.\",\n      \"While bias awareness helps, organization and accuracy are primary.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q612\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Recording emotional reactions to a poem in a reading journal reflects:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Critical evaluation\", \"Reader response\", \"Contextual decoding\", \"Summarization\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Critical evaluation analyzes objectively; emotional reactions are subjective.\",\n      \"Correct! Reader response emphasizes personal interpretation and emotional engagement.\",\n      \"Contextual decoding determines word meanings, not emotional reactions.\",\n      \"Summarization condenses content, not records reactions.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q613\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Writing across content areas enhances learning because it:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Reduces comprehension load\", \"Forces rote memorization\", \"Deepens understanding through reflection\", \"Eliminates the need for reading\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Writing increases cognitive engagement, not reduces it.\",\n      \"Writing to learn encourages reflection, not memorization.\",\n      \"Correct! Writing clarifies thinking, connects