nt style is aesthetic, not related to objectivity.\",\n      \"Sentence length affects readability, not objectivity.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q619\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which activity best builds metacognitive reading awareness?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Timed silent reading\", \"Think-aloud modeling\", \"Multiple-choice quizzes\", \"Copying vocabulary\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Silent reading doesn't explicitly demonstrate thinking processes.\",\n      \"Correct! Think-aloud modeling makes metacognitive strategies (monitoring, questioning, clarifying) visible and explicit.\",\n      \"Quizzes assess comprehension but don't build metacognitive awareness.\",\n      \"Copying vocabulary is rote practice, not metacognitive.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q620\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Comparing multiple interpretations of the same poem teaches students to:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Discredit authorial intent\", \"Appreciate diversity of meaning\", \"Memorize definitions\", \"Focus on grammar\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Multiple interpretations complement, not discredit, authorial intent.\",\n      \"Correct! Diverse interpretations validate reader response and show that meaning is constructed, not singular.\",\n      \"Comparing interpretations involves analysis, not memorization.\",\n      \"Grammar is not the focus of interpretation comparison.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q621\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"A student writing reflective journal entries about lab experiments is:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Practicing writing to learn\", \"Summarizing literature\", \"Composing persuasive essays\", \"Practicing syntax drills\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Writing to learn uses informal writing to process and reflect on content learning.\",\n      \"This is science content, not literature.\",\n      \"Reflective journals are exploratory, not persuasive.\",\n      \"Syntax drills are grammar exercises, not reflection.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q622\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Analyzing how a chart supports textual information demonstrates:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Visual interpretation\", \"Metaphorical reading\", \"Syntax comprehension\", \"Summative reasoning\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Visual interpretation extracts meaning from visual data representations like charts.\",\n      \"Metaphorical reading interprets figurative language, not charts.\",\n      \"Syntax comprehension analyzes sentence structure, not visual data.\",\n      \"Summative reasoning synthesizes ideas but isn't specific to visual data.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q623\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"When evaluating a website, which criterion is LEAST useful?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Design aesthetics\", \"Author's credentials\", \"Date of publication\", \"Evidence citations\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! While design matters for usability, aesthetics don't determine credibility or accuracy.\",\n      \"Author credentials indicate expertise and credibility—essential.\",\n      \"Publication date shows currency—important for time-sensitive topics.\",\n      \"Evidence citations demonstrate research quality—essential.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q624\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"The purpose of a public service announcement is primarily to:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Entertain\", \"Inform or persuade\", \"Amuse\", \"Narrate\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"PSAs can engage but entertainment isn't the primary purpose.\",\n      \"Correct! PSAs inform audiences about issues and/or persuade them toward action or behavior change.\",\n      \"Amusement may be a technique but not the goal.\",\n      \"Narration is a method, not a purpose.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q625\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"A speaker using celebrity endorsements to influence an audience uses which propaganda technique?\",\n    \"options\": [\"Transfer\", \"Testimonial\", \"Plain folks\", \"Fear appeal\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Transfer associates with symbols/concepts, not specific endorsers.\",\n      \"Correct! Testimonial uses endorsements from individuals (especially celebrities) to build credibility.\",\n      \"Plain folks presents speaker as ordinary person, opposite of celebrity.\",\n      \"Fear appeal uses threats or warnings, not endorsements.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q626\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Recognizing that film uses close-up shots to create empathy demonstrates:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Critical media analysis\", \"Visual confusion\", \"Phonemic awareness\", \"Literal comprehension\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Critical media analysis examines how technical choices (like shot selection) shape meaning and emotion.\",\n      \"This shows understanding, not confusion.\",\n      \"Phonemic awareness involves sound manipulation, not film analysis.\",\n      \"Literal comprehension understands surface meaning, not analyzes technique.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q627\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\n    \"stem\": \"Identifying how background music intensifies a news segment's emotional tone analyzes:\",\n    \"options\": [\"Nonverbal cues in media\", \"Logical appeals\", \"Morphological structure\", \"Syntax\"],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Nonverbal cues in media include music, sound effects, and visuals that shape emotional response.\",\n      \"Logical appeals use reasoning, not music.\",\n      \"Morphological structure relates to word formation, not media.\",\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not media production.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q628\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\n    \"stem\": \"A middle school teacher has students read excerpts from a famous speech before writing their own persuasive paragraphs on a modern topic. To strengthen the connection between reading and writing, the teacher asks students to analyze the rhetorical techniques used in the speech before they draft their own work. The primary benefit of this activity is that it helps students:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"memorize formal patterns of written and spoken argument.\",\n      \"transfer comprehension strategies from reading into composition.\",\n      \"imitate the stylistic conventions of historical texts.\",\n      \"focus on correct spelling and punctuation while writing.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Memorization is not the focus; understanding and application of rhetorical techniques is the goal.\",\n      \"Correct! Students are connecting analytical reading (how rhetoric works) with their own writing decisions—an integrated literacy practice.\",\n      \"Imitation is not the main goal; transferring analytical skills to composition is the focus.\",\n      \"Spelling and punctuation are not the primary focus of analyzing rhetorical techniques.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q629\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\n    \"stem\": \"After studying short stories with strong character development, students are asked to perform short dramatic readings of key scenes before writing a reflective essay about character motivation. The main advantage of this sequence is that it enables students to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"compare film and literature as forms of storytelling.\",\n      \"understand narrative pacing through silent reading.\",\n      \"internalize literary characterization through expressive performance.\",\n      \"demonstrate memorization skills for key literary passages.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Film comparison is not part of this activity; the focus is on dramatic reading and writing.\",\n      \"Silent reading is not the method used here; students are performing aloud.\",\n      \"Correct! Oral interpretation deepens comprehension and helps students feel how language shapes character and tone.\",\n      \"Memorization is not the goal; understanding character motivation through performance is the focus.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q630\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher plays an audio recording of a contemporary poet reading their own work, then asks students to analyze how the poet's vocal delivery affects meaning. Students then record themselves reading one of their own poems aloud. This assignment primarily promotes students' ability to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"evaluate how spoken language choices convey nuance and emotion.\",\n      \"identify phonemic awareness in a literary context.\",\n      \"assess the poet's adherence to standard pronunciation norms.\",\n      \"memorize and recite poetry for performance competition.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! It links listening and speaking to interpretation—core ELA integration.\",\n      \"Phonemic awareness relates to sound manipulation in language learning, not poetry analysis.\",\n      \"Standard pronunciation is not the focus; vocal delivery and meaning are the goals.\",\n      \"Memorization and competition are not the objectives; analysis and self-expression are the focus.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q631\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\n    \"stem\": \"Students in an English class maintain a weekly blog where they respond to class readings and comment on one another's posts. This activity most directly supports development in which area?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"grammar and sentence mechanics\",\n      \"collaborative writing and audience awareness\",\n      \"historical literary analysis\",\n      \"memorization of key vocabulary terms\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"While grammar may be practiced, it is not the primary focus of blogging and commenting.\",\n      \"Correct! Commenting and responding online merges reading, writing, and communication skills while reinforcing authentic audience awareness.\",\n      \"Historical literary analysis may occur but is not the main benefit of the blog activity.\",\n      \"Memorization is not a focus; the activity emphasizes interaction and communication.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q632\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\n    \"stem\": \"An English teacher asks students to create short podcasts explaining the themes of assigned novels and to include brief quotations to support their points. This approach best fosters:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"oral fluency through repeated reading practice.\",\n      \"integrated comprehension by synthesizing speaking, reading, and writing skills.\",\n      \"technical editing of recorded audio files.\",\n      \"memorization of literary quotations for assessment purposes.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Oral fluency may improve, but the main goal is integrating multiple literacy skills.\",\n      \"Correct! Students read to gather evidence, write to plan their script, and speak to communicate analysis—pure language arts integration.\",\n      \"Technical editing is a side skill, not the primary educational focus.\",\n      \"Memorization is not the goal; synthesis and communication of ideas are the focus.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q633\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"During the Old English period, the language was most heavily influenced by:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Latin brought by Roman missionaries\",\n      \"Norse vocabulary introduced through Viking contact\",\n      \"Celtic grammatical structures\",\n      \"Norman French syntax\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"While Latin influenced Old English through Christianity, it was not the most pervasive influence.\",\n      \"Correct! The Viking invasions infused Old English with Norse loanwords like 'sky,' 'egg,' and 'window,' shaping northern dialects.\",\n      \"Celtic had minimal impact on Old English grammar; most Celtic influence was place names.\",\n      \"Norman French came later during the Middle English period, not Old English.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q634\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which event most contributed to the standardization of English spelling and grammar?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"The Great Vowel Shift\",\n      \"The introduction of the printing press by William Caxton in 1476\",\n      \"The Protestant Reformation\",\n      \"The publication of Johnson's Dictionary of the English Language in 1755\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"The Great Vowel Shift changed pronunciation but did not standardize spelling.\",\n      \"Correct! The printing press made written English more uniform and spread London's dialect as a standard.\",\n      \"The Reformation influenced Bible translation but was not the primary standardization force.\",\n      \"Johnson's Dictionary was important but came after printing had already established many standards.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q635\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which development most clearly reflects the influence of global expansion on the English language?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"The adoption of words like café and rendezvous\",\n      \"The inclusion of loanwords from Hindi, Arabic, and Swahili\",\n      \"The simplification of English verb conjugations\",\n      \"The creation of Middle English poetic forms\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"French borrowings reflect European contact, not global expansion.\",\n      \"Correct! Colonial contact introduced vocabulary such as 'bungalow,' 'algebra,' and 'safari,' demonstrating English's global borrowing.\",\n      \"Verb simplification was an internal development, not due to global expansion.\",\n      \"Middle English poetry was a literary development, not related to global contact.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q636\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which factor most contributed to the divergence of British and American English spelling conventions?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"The establishment of colonial universities\",\n      \"Noah Webster's dictionary reforms\",\n      \"The Industrial Revolution\",\n      \"Early missionary translation projects\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Colonial universities preserved British conventions rather than diverging from them.\",\n      \"Correct! Webster intentionally simplified spellings—'color' vs. 'colour'—to promote linguistic independence in the new nation.\",\n      \"The Industrial Revolution affected vocabulary but not systematic spelling differences.\",\n      \"Missionary projects did not focus on spelling reform.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q637\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following best explains the rapid expansion of new vocabulary during the 20th century?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Growth of literacy and public schooling\",\n      \"Technological and scientific innovation\",\n      \"Simplification of English grammar\",\n      \"Increased translation of classical texts\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Literacy growth helped spread language but did not create new vocabulary.\",\n      \"Correct! Terms like 'television,' 'internet,' and 'genetic code' arose with modern inventions and discoveries.\",\n      \"Grammar simplification is unrelated to vocabulary expansion.\",\n      \"Classical translations added some terms but not at the pace of modern technology.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q638\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which statement best illustrates how social movements shape language?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Increased borrowing from Latin during the Renaissance\",\n      \"The emergence of gender-inclusive pronouns such as they for singular use\",\n      \"The creation of dictionaries to define 'standard' language\",\n      \"The replacement of Middle English endings with Modern English ones\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Latin borrowing was driven by scholarly interests, not social movements.\",\n      \"Correct! Contemporary social awareness influences evolving norms of identity and expression.\",\n      \"Dictionary creation reflected prescriptive authority, not social movements.\",\n      \"Grammar changes from Middle to Modern English were evolutionary, not movement-driven.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q639\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following is an example of English as a living, evolving language?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"The preservation of Old English in historical archives\",\n      \"The continued use of Shakespearean English in modern writing\",\n      \"The emergence of internet slang and new verb forms such as 'to google'\",\n      \"The adoption of prescriptive grammar handbooks in schools\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Preservation of Old English maintains history but does not show evolution.\",\n      \"Using Shakespearean English is archaic practice, not evolution.\",\n      \"Correct! Digital communication continuously produces new vocabulary and usage patterns.\",\n      \"Prescriptive handbooks attempt to freeze language, not demonstrate evolution.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q640\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which statement best reflects how regional dialects develop within a single language?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"They arise from deliberate educational reforms.\",\n      \"They result from isolation and distinct cultural contact patterns.\",\n      \"They emerge only in rural communities.\",\n      \"They reflect the random variation of individual speakers.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Educational reforms try to standardize, not create dialects.\",\n      \"Correct! Geography, migration, and community interaction create region-specific pronunciations and idioms.\",\n      \"Dialects develop in both urban and rural areas.\",\n      \"Dialect formation is systematic, not random individual variation.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q641\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"The English used by writers of the Harlem Renaissance (e.g., Langston Hughes, Zora Neale Hurston) demonstrates:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"rejection of African linguistic heritage\",\n      \"codification of formal standard English\",\n      \"creative adaptation of vernacular speech to convey cultural identity\",\n      \"resistance to all linguistic experimentation\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Harlem Renaissance writers embraced African linguistic heritage.\",\n      \"They challenged formal conventions rather than codifying them.\",\n      \"Correct! Writers blended African American vernacular with literary English to express cultural voice and authenticity.\",\n      \"Harlem Renaissance was marked by linguistic experimentation, not resistance to it.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q642\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\n    \"stem\": \"In contemporary linguistics, the term World Englishes refers to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"Attempts to restore classical grammar in global education\",\n      \"Localized varieties of English shaped by regional and cultural factors\",\n      \"The replacement of native languages with English worldwide\",\n      \"Simplified English used exclusively in diplomacy\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"World Englishes recognizes diversity, not restoration of classical grammar.\",\n      \"Correct! 'World Englishes' recognizes that British, Indian, Nigerian, and Singaporean English are all legitimate, evolving varieties.\",\n      \"The term celebrates multilingualism, not language replacement.\",\n      \"World Englishes includes all varieties, not just simplified diplomatic English.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q643\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher organizes a literature unit including The Joy Luck Club by Amy Tan, Bless Me, Ultima by Rudolfo Anaya, and The Namesake by Jhumpa Lahiri. The teacher's primary purpose in selecting these works is to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"expose students to novels written exclusively in first person.\",\n      \"highlight the influence of myth and folklore in contemporary fiction.\",\n      \"explore the experiences of cultural identity and assimilation in the United States.\",\n      \"teach students how to apply postmodern literary theory.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"While some may use first person, narrative perspective is not the unifying theme.\",\n      \"Myth and folklore appear but are not the primary focus across all three novels.\",\n      \"Correct! Each novel portrays generational and cultural negotiation—ideal for discussing identity and assimilation.\",\n      \"Postmodern theory is not the main instructional goal of this selection.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q644\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\n    \"stem\": \"A high-school class reads The Diary of Anne Frank alongside Night by Elie Wiesel. The teacher's goal is most likely to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"demonstrate different approaches to narrative structure.\",\n      \"examine personal and historical perspectives on human rights and moral courage.\",\n      \"introduce students to post-World War II literary techniques.\",\n      \"analyze translation differences between European and American editions.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"While structure varies, it is not the primary educational purpose of pairing these texts.\",\n      \"Correct! Pairing survivor accounts helps students connect literature to ethical and historical understanding.\",\n      \"Literary techniques are secondary to the human rights and moral themes.\",\n      \"Translation analysis is not the focus of this pairing.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q645\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\n    \"stem\": \"An English teacher assigns Their Eyes Were Watching God and asks students to compare it with modern spoken-word poetry exploring similar themes. This assignment primarily helps students:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"practice formal citation in research essays.\",\n      \"identify social commentary on gender and voice across time periods.\",\n      \"memorize dialect for oral performance.\",\n      \"evaluate publishing trends in African American literature.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Citation practice is a skill outcome, not the primary purpose of thematic comparison.\",\n      \"Correct! It links historical and contemporary expressions of autonomy and voice—illustrating literature's social purpose.\",\n      \"Memorization of dialect is not the instructional goal.\",\n      \"Publishing trends are not the focus of this thematic comparison.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q646\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher selects several environmental poems and essays, such as those by Mary Oliver and Wendell Berry, for a thematic unit titled 'Literature of Place.' The main instructional purpose of this unit is to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"demonstrate scientific accuracy in literary description.\",\n      \"connect students' reading to ecological awareness and personal reflection.\",\n      \"teach metrical variation in free verse poetry.\",\n      \"focus on the economic factors influencing rural literature.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Scientific accuracy is not the primary goal of literary study.\",\n      \"Correct! It integrates literature with environmental ethics and reflective thinking.\",\n      \"Meter is a technical element, not the main purpose of this thematic unit.\",\n      \"Economic factors are not the central focus of environmental literature study.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q647\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\n    \"stem\": \"During a world-literature course, students read Things Fall Apart by Chinua Achebe and discuss colonial perspectives portrayed in early British novels. The teacher's intent in this comparison is to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"reinforce grammar and vocabulary from non-Western sources.\",\n      \"expose students to non-Western viewpoints and post-colonial themes.\",\n      \"illustrate the chronological development of the English novel.\",\n      \"teach students translation methods for African languages.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Grammar and vocabulary are not the primary focus of this comparison.\",\n      \"Correct! Achebe's work reclaims narrative authority, showing literature's power to challenge dominant cultural perspectives.\",\n      \"Chronological development is secondary to examining cultural perspectives.\",\n      \"Translation methods are not the goal of this comparative study.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q648\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\n    \"stem\": \"In Wuthering Heights, Emily Brontë's use of multiple narrators and a non-linear structure primarily serves to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"create a sense of historical distance between reader and character.\",\n      \"emphasize the power of social hierarchy in determining fate.\",\n      \"present differing psychological perspectives that complicate moral judgment.\",\n      \"highlight the influence of classical myth on Romantic storytelling.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"While historical distance exists, the primary purpose is psychological complexity, not temporal separation.\",\n      \"Social hierarchy is a theme, but the narrative structure's main function is to present multiple viewpoints.\",\n      \"Correct! Brontë's framing devices (Lockwood and Nelly) invite readers to weigh conflicting viewpoints, deepening the novel's moral ambiguity.\",\n      \"Classical myth is not the primary influence on Brontë's narrative technique.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q649\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\n    \"stem\": \"Elizabeth Gaskell's North and South explores industrialization and class conflict in Victorian England. Her portrayal of these social tensions is most significant because it:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"romanticizes the lives of working-class characters to promote idealism.\",\n      \"integrates social realism with moral sympathy to critique economic inequality.\",\n      \"rejects political engagement in favor of domestic harmony.\",\n      \"portrays the decline of rural England through comic irony.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Gaskell presents realistic, not romanticized, portrayals of working-class life.\",\n      \"Correct! Gaskell's realist approach humanizes labor issues and bridges moral and social commentary—an early example of industrial fiction.\",\n      \"The novel actively engages with political and economic issues rather than avoiding them.\",\n      \"Comic irony is not the dominant tone; Gaskell employs earnest social realism.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q650\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\n    \"stem\": \"Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre was groundbreaking among nineteenth-century novels because it:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"portrayed a heroine who rejects both love and independence.\",\n      \"depicted a female protagonist whose moral and emotional autonomy challenge Victorian gender norms.\",\n      \"idealized romantic dependence as a form of social stability.\",\n      \"dismissed religious and moral struggle as irrelevant to personal growth.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Jane seeks both love and independence on her own terms, not rejecting either.\",\n      \"Correct! Jane's insistence on self-respect and conscience makes the novel an early feminist and psychological narrative.\",\n      \"The novel critiques, rather than idealizes, romantic dependence that compromises autonomy.\",\n      \"Religious and moral struggle are central to Jane's character development throughout the novel.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q651\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\n    \"stem\": \"Emily Brontë's depiction of nature in Wuthering Heights differs from typical Romantic literature because it:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"presents the natural world as indifferent or violent rather than purely inspiring.\",\n      \"replaces imagery of rural beauty with industrial landscapes.\",\n      \"idealizes pastoral life as an escape from moral decay.\",\n      \"omits natural imagery to focus entirely on domestic interiors.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Correct! Her wild moorland setting mirrors the intensity of human passion and cruelty—nature as emotional landscape, not idyllic refuge.\",\n      \"The novel is set on the moors, not in industrial landscapes.\",\n      \"Brontë subverts pastoral idealization by presenting nature as harsh and unforgiving.\",\n      \"Natural imagery is central to the novel's atmosphere and symbolism.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q652\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\n    \"stem\": \"In Mary Barton, Elizabeth Gaskell combines sentimental narrative techniques with journalistic observation to:\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"disguise political content beneath conventional romance.\",\n      \"argue that industrial progress inevitably leads to social harmony.\",\n      \"appeal to middle-class readers' empathy for working-class suffering.\",\n      \"portray factory life as an entertaining backdrop for personal ambition.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"Rather than disguising political content, Gaskell makes it central and explicit through empathetic storytelling.\",\n      \"The novel critiques, not celebrates, industrial progress without social reform.\",\n      \"Correct! Gaskell uses empathy and realism to awaken social conscience among her readership—hallmarks of early social-problem fiction.\",\n      \"Factory life is portrayed seriously as a site of suffering, not entertainment.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q653\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"English syntax and sentence structure\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences violates the conventions of English syntax?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"The candle flickered softly in the wind.\",\n      \"Down the stairs fell the book with a thud.\",\n      \"They the park quickly across ran.\",\n      \"The dog barked loudly at the mail carrier.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"This follows standard subject–verb–object order with proper syntax.\",\n      \"This uses inverted word order for emphasis, which is grammatically acceptable in English.\",\n      \"Correct! 'They the park quickly across ran' scrambles normal English word order (subject–verb–object) and violates syntax conventions.\",\n      \"This follows standard English syntax with subject, verb, and adverbial phrase.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 1\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q654\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Parallel structure and sentence coherence\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which sentence demonstrates faulty parallel structure?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"The hikers were cold, tired, and hungry for dinner.\",\n      \"She enjoys dancing, to swim, and playing tennis.\",\n      \"He came, he saw, he conquered.\",\n      \"The speech was long but inspiring.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"This maintains parallel structure with three adjectives in a series.\",\n      \"Correct! Parallel structure requires consistent grammatical forms — this should be 'dancing, swimming, and playing' (all gerunds) or 'to dance, to swim, and to play' (all infinitives).\",\n      \"This demonstrates excellent parallel structure with three parallel clauses.\",\n      \"This uses parallel adjectives to describe the speech.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q655\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Sentence fragments and complete sentences\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences contains a sentence fragment?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"After the rain stopped, the children ran outside.\",\n      \"Because she was late to the meeting.\",\n      \"The bus left at noon sharp.\",\n      \"The teacher collected the homework on time.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"This is a complete sentence with a dependent clause followed by an independent clause.\",\n      \"Correct! 'Because she was late to the meeting' is a dependent clause missing its main clause. It needs an independent clause to be complete.\",\n      \"This is a complete sentence with subject, verb, and additional details.\",\n      \"This is a complete sentence with subject, verb, and object.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q656\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Modifiers and clarity\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences demonstrates a misplaced modifier?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"She nearly drove her car for ten hours without stopping.\",\n      \"The teacher handed the papers to the students on the desk.\",\n      \"Running quickly, the child caught the ball.\",\n      \"After lunch, the meeting resumed promptly.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"While 'nearly' could be clearer if moved, the sentence meaning is relatively clear.\",\n      \"Correct! 'On the desk' incorrectly modifies 'students,' implying they were sitting on the desk rather than the papers being on the desk. The modifier is misplaced.\",\n      \"The modifier 'Running quickly' correctly modifies 'the child.'\",\n      \"The modifier 'After lunch' correctly modifies when the meeting resumed.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"Q657\",\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\n    \"objective\": \"Structural ambiguity and coordination\",\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences is structurally ambiguous because of faulty coordination?\",\n    \"options\": [\n      \"The coach praised the players, and the crowd cheered loudly.\",\n      \"She told him she loved her cat and so did he.\",\n      \"The storm passed quickly and left the sky clear.\",\n      \"He wanted to visit London and Paris during his trip.\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"rationales\": [\n      \"This has clear coordination between two independent clauses with no ambiguity.\",\n      \"Correct! 'She told him she loved her cat and so did he' could mean he also told her, or that he also loved her cat — structural ambiguity through coordination.\",\n      \"This clearly coordinates two actions performed by the storm.\",\n      \"This clearly coordinates two cities he wanted to visit.\"\n    ],\n    \"difficulty\": 2\n  }\n]\n;\n});","lineCount":4374,"map":[[4374,3]],"functionMap":null},"type":"js/module"}]}